cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below, and cite the references listed in the README (in particular Madej et al. 2017 for the Compton kernel and Gunasekera et al. 2025 for Cloudy)."
title: "DAO — X-ray Reflection Spectroscopy Model"
abstract: >-
  X-ray reflection spectra probe the inner accretion disks of black holes,
  but differences in model predictions introduce uncertainties in their
  interpretation. We present DAO, an open-source reflection model combining
  Cloudy atomic physics with a fully angle- and energy-dependent Compton
  redistribution kernel that retains the directional information lost in
  angle-averaged treatments. DAO recovers the cold reflection continuum of
  pexrav in the Compton-hump energy band and the inclination-dependent
  Comptonized spectra of compPS. For illumination with Γ = 2,
  E_cut = 300 keV, and μ_inc ≈ 0.8, the same calculation with an
  angle-averaged kernel yields reflected intensities at 100 keV that are
  14–16% higher than the angle-dependent solution at each of four viewing
  angles. In a high-density, highly ionized atmosphere
  (n_H = 10^18 cm^-3, log ξ = 3), the O VIII Lyα peak is closer to
  reflionx than to xillverCp, whose peak is approximately 4.4 times higher
  relative to the local continuum. Removing the escape-probability
  suppression of line emission and recomputing the atmosphere raises the
  DAO line peak by only 7.5%, so this suppression cannot account for the
  factor-of-four difference. A direct comparison of Cloudy and XSTAR shows
  much larger differences in O VIII emission and gas temperature at
  n_H = 10^18 than at 10^15 cm^-3, suggesting that differences in
  high-density atomic treatment may contribute to the discrepancy.
  The code enables controlled tests of such physical differences and is
  designed to be extended to polarized reflection and a self-consistent
  vertical disk structure.
type: software
authors:
